{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","count":22,"questions":[{"id":51335,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A mineral consists of a cubic close-packed structure formed by $\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ ions where half the octahedral voids are occupied by $\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ and oneeighth of the tetrahedral voids are occupied by $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$. The chemical formula of the mineral is","options":["$\\mathrm{Mn}_{3} \\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$","$\\mathrm{MnAl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{MnAl}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$","$\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"No of $\\mathrm{O}^{-2}$ per unit cell $=8 \\times \\frac{1}{8}+6 \\times \\frac{1}{2}=4$\nNo of $\\mathrm{Al}^{+3}$ per unit cell $=4 \\times \\frac{1}{2}=2$\nNo of $\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}$ per unit cell $=8 \\times \\frac{1}{8}=1\\mathbf{M n A l}_{2} \\mathbf{O}_{4}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21342599fdbdefd92b7"},{"id":51336,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A two-dimensional solid pattern formed by two different atoms $X$ and $Y$ is shown below. The black and white squares represent atoms $\\mathrm{X}$ and $\\mathrm{Y}$, respectively. The simplest formula for the compound based on the unit cell from the pattern is\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FU9D0xD_WG-EgJY7SCZ9x1rFlR20-m371QTz-Qny723Y.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FU9D0xD_WG-EgJY7SCZ9x1rFlR20-m371QTz-Qny723Y.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FU9D0xD_WG-EgJY7SCZ9x1rFlR20-m371QTz-Qny723Y.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\mathrm{XY}_{8}$","$X_{4} Y_{9}$","$\\mathrm{XY}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{XY}_{4}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"The unit cell of the above pattern will consist of 8 white square and 1 black square i.e. it will form centre unit cell.\n$\\therefore$ No. of white square $\\mathrm{Y}=8$\nNo. of black square $X=1\\therefore$ Formula $\\mathbf{X Y}_{\\mathbf{8}}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2132e18b62eb0102190"},{"id":51337,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Polonium (atomic mass = 209) crystallizes in a simple cubic structure with a density of $9.32 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$. Its lattice parameter (in $\\mathrm{pm}$ ) is closest to","options":["421","334","481","193"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{d}=\\frac{\\mathrm{N} \\times \\mathrm{M}}{\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\times \\mathrm{a}^{3}}9.32=\\frac{1 \\times 209}{6.023 \\times 10^{23} \\times \\mathrm{a}^{3}}\\therefore \\mathrm{a}^{3}=37.2 \\times 10^{-24 \\mathrm{~S}}\\mathrm{a}=3.33 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{~cm}\\cong 334 \\mathrm{pm}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2132e2ebcd124a98894"},{"id":51338,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The packing efficiency in a body-centred cubic (bcc) structure is closet to","options":["$74 \\%$","$63 \\%$","$68 \\%$","$52 \\%$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\eta=\\frac{2 \\times 4 / 3 \\pi R^{3}}{\\left(\\frac{4 \\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^{3}} \\times 100 \\cong 68 \\%$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d862c51fcec0de8e"},{"id":51339,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A compound is formed by two elements <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> forms hexagonal closed pack array with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math> of the octahedral holes occupied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The formula of the compound is:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"Number of atoms of element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>'</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>'</mo></math> per unit cell<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math><br />\nNumber of atoms of element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> per unit cell<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> (Number of octahedral voids per unit cell)<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math><br />\nFormula is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da749e6b9e70141aad775"},{"id":51340,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Schottky defect in a crystal arises due to","options":["creation of equal number of cation and anion vacancies","creation of unequal number of cation and anion vacancies","migration of cations to interstitial voids","migration of anions to interstitial voids"],"answer":0,"solution":"In SCHOTTKY by defect equal number of cations and anions leave the crystal LATTICE to form equal number of cationic and anionic vacancies.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213ac5a8083e864e586"},{"id":51341,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The packing efficiency of the face centered cubic (fcc), body centered cubic (bcc) and simple / primitive cubic (pc) lattices follows the order","options":["$\\mathrm{fcc}\\gt \\mathrm{bcc}\\gt \\mathrm{pc}$","$b c c\\gt f c c\\gt p c$","$\\mathrm{pc}\\gt \\mathrm{bcc}\\gt \\mathrm{fcc}$","$b c c\\gt p c\\gt f c c$"],"answer":0,"solution":"\\begin{array}{|l|l|l|l|}\n\\hline & FCC & BCC & SC \\\\\n\\hline\\eta & 74 \\% & 68 \\% & 52.4 \\% \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\norder $\\mathrm{FCC}\\gt \\mathrm{BCC}\\gt \\mathrm{SC}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d836cf762037052e"},{"id":51342,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A solid is hard and brittle. It is an insulator in solid state, but conducts electricity in molten state. The solid is a","options":["molecular solid","ionic solid","metallic solid","covalent solid"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Characteristics given are that of ionic solids.</p>\n\n<p>Hard, brittle, insulator as solid and conductor in molten state.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f63ac9f320dd8ae09f"},{"id":51343,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"When $1.88 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{AgBr}(\\mathrm{s})$ is added to a $10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{KBr}$, the concentration of $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$is $5 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{M}$. if the same amount of $\\mathrm{AgBr}(\\mathrm{s})$ is added to a $10^{-2} \\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO} 3$, the concentration of $\\mathrm{Br}$ is:","options":["$9.4 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{M}$","$5 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{M}$","$1 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{M}$","$5 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{M}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{SP}} \\mathrm{AgBr}=\\left(\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right)(\\mathrm{Br})=\\left(5 \\times 10^{-10}\\right)\\left(10^{-3}\\right)=5 \\times 10^{-13}$\nConcentration of $\\mathrm{Br}$ in $10^{-2} \\mathrm{M}$ aq $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Ksp} \\mathrm{AgBr}}{\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right]}$\n=\\frac{5 \\times 10^{-13}}{\\left(10^{-2}\\right)}=5 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{~m}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213458c8c6ee2a4c9b7"},{"id":51344,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In a cubic close packed structure, fractional contributions of an atom at the corner and at the face in the unit cell are, respectively:","options":["$1 / 8$ and $1 / 2$","$1 / 2$ and $1 / 4$","$1 / 4$ and $1 / 2$","$1 / 4$ and $1 / 8$"],"answer":0,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136df608fbd1bd7d2e","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51345,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An ionic compound is formed between a metal M and a non-metal Y. If M occupies half the octahedral voids in the cubic close-packed arrangement formed by $\\mathrm{Y}$, the chemical formula of the ionic compound is","options":["MY","$\\mathrm{MY}_{2}$","$M_{2} Y$","$\\mathrm{MY}_{3}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FHD3DD_u-r-w6Z_G95zSd1PT2J_nSPD24oCRfJAxNvKU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FHD3DD_u-r-w6Z_G95zSd1PT2J_nSPD24oCRfJAxNvKU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FHD3DD_u-r-w6Z_G95zSd1PT2J_nSPD24oCRfJAxNvKU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nthen formula of ionic compound is $\\mathrm{MY}_{2}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137d40036f9365ada7"},{"id":51346,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Two elements, $X$ and $Y$, have atomic numbers 33 and 17 respectively. The milecular formula of a stable compound formed between then is","options":["$X Y$","$X Y_{2}$","$X Y_{3}$","$X Y_{4}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Compound is $\\mathrm{AsCl}_{3}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136f649d8e91511976"},{"id":51347,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The radius of $\\mathrm{K}^{+}$is $133 \\mathrm{pm}$ and that of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-\\text {is }} 181 \\mathrm{pm}$. The volume of the unit cell of $\\mathrm{KCl}$ expressed in $10^{-22}\\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ is","options":["$0.31$","$1.21$","$2.48$","$6.28$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\quad \\mathrm{V}_{k+}+\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{Cl}}=133+181\\mathrm{KCl}$ form FCC Lattice\n$\\therefore 2\\left(V_{\\mathrm{V}}=\\mathrm{a}^{\\mathrm{K}^{+}}+\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{Cl}-}\\right)=\\mathrm{a}=$ edge length of unit cell","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213878a80a6f47edb25"},{"id":51348,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"To obtain a diffraction peak, for a crystalline solid with interplane distance equal to the wavelength of incident $X$-ray radiation, the angle of incidence should be","options":["$90^{\\circ}$","$0^{\\circ}$","$30^{\\circ}$","$60^{\\circ}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"From Bragg's equation\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{n} \\lambda=2 \\mathrm{~d} \\sin \\theta \\\\\n\\mathrm{d}=\\lambda, \\mathrm{n}=1 \\\\\n1 \\times \\lambda=2 \\times \\lambda \\sin \\theta \\\\\n\\sin \\theta=\\frac{1}{-} \\\\\n\\theta=30^{\\circ}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139c3ea7f35ee5701b"},{"id":51349,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Doping silicon with boron produces a-","options":["n-type semiconductor","Metallic conductor","p-type semiconductor","Insulator"],"answer":2,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213af07cadefbab10df","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51350,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A metal with an atomic radius of $141.4 \\mathrm{pm}$ crystallizes in the face centred cubic structure. The volume of the unit cell in pm is-","options":["$2.74 \\times 10^{7}$","$2.19 \\times 10^{7}$","$6.40 \\times 10^{7}$","$9.20 \\times 10^{7}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\quad \\mathrm{r}=\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{2 \\sqrt{2}}=141.4 \\mathrm{pm}$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{a}=2 \\times \\sqrt{2} \\times 141.4 \\\\\n\\therefore \\mathrm{V}=\\mathrm{a}^{3}=(2 \\times \\sqrt{2} \\times 141.4)^{3}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213c9da374549bc642c"},{"id":51351,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In metallicids, the number of atoms for the face-centered and the body-centered cubic unit cells, are, respectively-","options":["2,4","2,2","4,2","4,4"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\quad \\mathrm{Fcc}=8 \\times \\frac{1}{8}+6 \\times \\frac{1}{2}=4$\n\\mathrm{Bcc}=8 \\times \\frac{1}{8}+1 \\times 1=2\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21356ffbc9e33504d23"},{"id":51352,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A two dimensional solid is made by alternating circles with radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></math> such that the sides of the circles touch. The packing fraction is defined as the ratio of the area under the circles to the area under the rectangle with sides of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F6cc5a046-1075-400e-b934-fb1bd9c65790-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6cc5a046-1075-400e-b934-fb1bd9c65790-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6cc5a046-1075-400e-b934-fb1bd9c65790-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 399px; height: 111px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></math> for which the packing fraction is minimized is closest to:</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>41</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>50</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>32</mn></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F73c78ba7-27e9-4c9e-b755-65a624130eda-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F73c78ba7-27e9-4c9e-b755-65a624130eda-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F73c78ba7-27e9-4c9e-b755-65a624130eda-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 201px; height: 118px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>Area of rectangle<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>xy</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>Area covered by circles<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>πa</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>πb</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>Packing fraction (P.F.)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ab</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>πa</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p>Putting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mfrac></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p>For minimum P.F., <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>dr</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>414</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>Answer is option (A)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da74db7e8cc602bc92ea3"},{"id":51353,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Copper (atomic mass $=63.5$ ) crystallizers in a FCC lattice and has density $8.93 \\mathrm{~g} . \\mathrm{cm}^{-3} .$ The radius of copper atom is closest to","options":["$361.6 \\mathrm{pm}$","$511.4 \\mathrm{pm}$","$127.8 \\mathrm{pm}$","$102.8 \\mathrm{pm}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{d}=\\frac{\\mathrm{N} \\times \\mathrm{M}}{\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\times \\mathrm{a}^{3}}8.93=\\frac{4 \\times 63.5}{6.023 \\times 10^{23} \\times \\mathrm{a}^{3}}\\mathrm{a}^{3}=4.72 \\times 10^{-23}\\mathrm{a}=\\left(47.2 \\times 10^{-24}\\right)^{1 / 3}=3.61 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{~cm}=3.61 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~m}=361 \\mathrm{pm}$ force $\\mathrm{a}=2 \\sqrt{2} \\mathrm{r}\\mathrm{r}=\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{2 \\sqrt{2}}=127.8$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137d2c7d20c08838bd"},{"id":51354,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A unit cell of calcium fluoride has four calcium ions. The number of fluoride ions in the unit cell is","options":["2","4","6","8"],"answer":3,"solution":"No. of $F^{-}$will be equal to eight since for one $\\mathrm{Ca}^{+2}$ there should be two $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$ion.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139a4aa4e01fcc0edb"},{"id":51355,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Atoms of an element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></math> form hexagonal closed packed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>hcp</mi></mfenced></math> lattice and atoms of element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> occupy all the tetrahedral voids. The formula of the compound is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>XZ</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XZ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Number of lattice points in a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>hcp</mi></math> lattice : number of tetrahedral voids is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math> ratio. </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></math> atoms : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> atoms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Formula of compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da707318f9a2460cf2c67"},{"id":51356,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solid State","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The inter-planar spacing between the ( 221$)$ planes of a cubic lattice of length $450 \\mathrm{pm}$ is $-$","options":["$50 \\mathrm{pm}$","$150 \\mathrm{pm}$","$300 \\mathrm{pm}$","$450 \\mathrm{pm}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{d}_{\\mathrm{hkl}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{h}^{2}+\\mathrm{k}^{2}+1^{2}}} \\\\\n=\\frac{450}{\\sqrt{4+4+1}}=\\frac{450}{\\sqrt{9}}=150 \\mathrm{pm}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213714db17a34e32075"}]}